WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:05.400
If you were to define what it meant to be&nbsp;
a banana, you had to tick every box on that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:10.980
list and then you could be nothing but a banana.&nbsp;
We're going to look at a sequence and show that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:16.560
it converges to a limit you weren't expecting. So&nbsp;
the sequence is as follows: the first term is 9,

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.960
second term is 99, third term 999.&nbsp;
Fourth term - you guessed it - four 9s;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:27.060
and we're going to continue with this pattern.&nbsp;
So obviously this sequence goes to infinity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:33.240
We're adding more and more 9s, so in the&nbsp;
usual sense this is a divergent sequence;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:39.240
it goes to infinity, it's getting bigger.
But I'm going to show you that in fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:45.720
this tends to -1. This sequence of adding more&nbsp;
and more 9s, making the number larger and larger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:55.080
in a certain situation can be shown to tend to -1.
And here's how it works. First of all we need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.080 --> 00:01:00.900
establish what we mean by saying a sequence tends&nbsp;
to a particular number. This might seem like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:06.120
something you know, or you've seen before, but&nbsp;
this is going to be really important in justifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:13.080
why this is -1 so bear with me. So if we start&nbsp;
with a different sequence; so suppose I have 3/2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:21.600
4/3, 5/4, 6/5 and it's going to continue. So&nbsp;
we're adding 1 to each of the numerators - numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.560
on top - and we're adding 1 to each number on&nbsp;
the bottom. So the nth term, if we would sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:30.180
of go along and say pick a certain point in the&nbsp;
sequence, what does the nth term look like at that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:37.980
position? It's going to be n plus 2 over n plus&nbsp;
1. So first term, plug it in, we get 3 on the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:44.640
over 2. Third term here this would be n equals 3,&nbsp;
we get 5 on top 4 on the bottom. Whatever position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.500
down you might say to me, find the 1000th&nbsp;
term in the sequence, it's a 1002 over 1001.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:55.980
Now this hopefully you can see is going to tend
to 1. As we increase n, we go further and further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:59.700
along the sequence, the top and the bottom&nbsp;
get closer and closer together. So we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:03.420
get as close to 1 as we want by going&nbsp;
further and further down the sequence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:09.300
So the way we would show that is you take&nbsp;
your nth term - which here is n plus 2 over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:14.940
n plus 1 - and we subtract off what we think the&nbsp;
limit is, so here we think it's going to be 1,&nbsp;

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:21.960
and then we work out the distance between these&nbsp;
two. So I'm calling this our distance function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:26.940
Now if we're working in the real numbers - which&nbsp;
these are all real numbers - then the distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:33.000
function is just the modulus of one number&nbsp;
subtract the other. So we take the modulus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.140
because it's a distance so it should be positive&nbsp;
- you can't really have a negative distance. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:42.900
here the distance in the real numbers is n&nbsp;
plus 2 over n plus 1 minus 1. What is n plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:49.200
2 over n plus 1? Well we can simplify that&nbsp;
and say, well that's actually the same as n&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:55.080
plus 1 plus another 1 all over n plus 1 minus 1.
But then we've got an n plus 1 over an n plus 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:00.720
so that's a 1 so we can say that's actually 1 plus&nbsp;
1 over n plus 1 minus 1 so we get 1 over n plus 1.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:12.720
So again, with this distance function the usual&nbsp;
way of measuring distance, we can show that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.460
nth term of the sequence and what we think the&nbsp;
limit is; we can say the distance between them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:22.740
looks like 1 over n plus 1. So as we now go&nbsp;
further and further down the sequence what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:29.220
we're doing is we're letting n tend to infinity.&nbsp;
We're making n bigger and bigger, what happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:36.300
to our distance as n gets bigger, the distance&nbsp;
tends to zero. So as n tends to infinity, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:42.900
distance between the two things is 1 over n plus 1&nbsp;
which very clearly tends to zero, so we would say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:49.020
this sequence converges to 1. And we've done that&nbsp;
using the distance function, which for the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:57.660
numbers is just modulus of one of them minus the&nbsp;
other. I'm going to need more paper. As&nbsp;always.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:03.660
The reason I've introduced the distance&nbsp;
function, as some of you may have guessed, is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:10.440
if we're going to show our sequence of 9s&nbsp;
is tending to -1 we obviously can't use the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:16.740
normal definition of distance because the distance&nbsp;
between a larger larger number and -1 is getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:22.500
bigger so it's it's just not going to work, it's&nbsp;
not going to tend to zero. So we need to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.180
about what other definition of distance can we use&nbsp;
to show that this sequence is indeed going to -1.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:39.060
And the one we use is called the 2-adic metric -&nbsp;
and we will check this is a distance. You say the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:45.120
distance between two numbers x and y. So x and&nbsp;
y are going to be integers because here in our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:49.200
sequence we're only working with integers so we'll&nbsp;
keep things simple. So the distance between two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:05:00.420
integers in this metric is defined to be 1 divided&nbsp;
by 2 to the m, where m is the largest power of 2&nbsp;

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:07.260
that divides x minus y. So this feels a little&nbsp;
complicated, so let's look at an example, let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:15.780
think about what does this mean. Let's suppose&nbsp;
x is 100 and y is 4. So in the usual measure of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:24.240
distance we would say the modulus of x minus y&nbsp;
is the modulus of 100 minus 4, they're 96 apart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:31.380
Feels hopefully kind of obvious. Now in the 2-adic
metric we've changed our definition of distance,

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:37.500
so we need to think about what power of 2 divides&nbsp;
their difference. So we already know the difference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:46.140
the difference between them is 96. So if we think&nbsp;
of powers of 2 we've got 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64. 128 is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:53.940
obviously too big so I'm going to stop there. 64,&nbsp;
well that's 2 to the 6, but 64 doesn't divide 96.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:59.220
It's smaller than it but 64 times 2 - which would&nbsp;
be the next smallest thing you could have - wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:07.740
give you 96. So we actually need to go down to&nbsp;
2 to the 5, which is 32, which perfectly divides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:15.660
96. So 2 to the 5 is 32, 32 times 3 gives us 96.&nbsp;
2 to the 5 divides the difference between our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:26.280
numbers so, in our definition, we can say in the&nbsp;
2-adic metric the distance between 100 and 4 is 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:32.700
over 2 to the biggest power, so here 2 to the&nbsp;
5, so that's going to be 1 over 32. So usual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:39.060
definition of distance that we're all used to,&nbsp;
these numbers are 96 apart, 100 and 4. However in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:49.140
metric they are 1 over 32 apart; which doesn't&nbsp;
feel at all natural and-
- (Brady: Or useful)
- Or useful. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:53.940
mean well that's a whole other conversation.&nbsp;
We're entering the realm of pure maths. This-

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:58.680
now that you've set me off on this - it is useful&nbsp;
because we learn properties about the numbers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:07.260
So this kind of Investigation, okay this isn't&nbsp;
a distance in our world, but we can learn things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:13.200
about distributions of numbers through this. Ao&nbsp;
this 2-adic metric can be generalised and is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:21.000
used to actually in fields medal level research&nbsp;
to learn about rational numbers. So beyond beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:25.920
my pay grade, beyond my ability to do the&nbsp;
maths, but there is some usefulness in there.

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:35.100
So we've got our 2-adic metric. So I've just&nbsp;
told you this is a way of figuring out how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:42.720
far apart two numbers are, but you need to check&nbsp;
from a mathematical perspective that it does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:48.900
everything you would expect a distance measure&nbsp;
to do. So we have a set of axioms. In order to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:55.800
a distance function: it maps from two numbers in&nbsp;
our starting space into the real numbers, we get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:01.920
an answer that's a real number. Where d satisfies&nbsp;
three things - this one's called positivity. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:06.420
these should hopefully fit with your intuition&nbsp;
of what we might expect from a distance. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:11.880
we have two numbers x and y, the distance&nbsp;
between them is greater than or equal to zero.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:17.400
We're talking about distances, okay they might&nbsp;
not fit our usual way we think about them, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:22.380
they still need to be positive. You can't have&nbsp;
a non-positive distance, negative distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:29.340
makes no sense. And in fact you can say and it&nbsp;
equals zero if and only if x is equal to y. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:33.480
you only have a zero distance if it's the same&nbsp;
point. Again, hopefully fits with our usual notion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:39.180
Second one is called symmetry, so this says the&nbsp;
distance between x and y- so if I start with x&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:43.260
and move to a point y, I should obviously get&nbsp;
the same thing if I start at y and move back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:48.540
to x. d of x,y is d of y,x - quite straightforward.
Doesn't matter where we start. The third one is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:54.300
called the triangle law. Now this one is best seen&nbsp;
by picture; if I start at point x and I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.300 --> 00:09:00.480
go to point z then what this says is moving&nbsp;
directly from x to z is always going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:08.280
faster than going via some third point y.&nbsp;
Again, fits our usual way of thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:13.560
distances. If you're thinking about the usual&nbsp;
measure of distance this is saying the shortest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:18.720
way to move between two points in the plane is a&nbsp;
straight line. Of course it changes if you're on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:23.940
spheres and changing your geometry. In terms of our&nbsp;
function that says the distance between x and z&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:33.580
has to be less than or equal to - so it's shorter&nbsp;
than - going by a detour. So if we did d(x,y) plus d(y,z)

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:39.060
written out algebraically. So these three axioms,&nbsp;
three rules, need to be satisfied in order for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:44.940
a function d to be called a distance in terms&nbsp;
of the mathematical definition of a distance. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:51.960
we just need to check that our 2-adic metric&nbsp;
satisfies these three things; because at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:57.000
point you're just taking my word that this is&nbsp;
a reasonable thing to do, even though we get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:02.340
rather ridiculous answer. So we do want to verify&nbsp;
this. Let's write down our definition, 1 over 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:07.560
to the m where the m was the largest power of 2&nbsp;
that divides their difference. So is this positive?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:12.600
It's one over a power of 2, that's certainly a&nbsp;
positive number. So we can say that works. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:18.240
all good. Symmetry: if I do the distance between x&nbsp;
and y, is that the same as doing y and x? Well here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:27.360
we were finding the largest power of 2 that&nbsp;
divided x minus y. So what that means is we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:34.740
saying 2 to the m divides x minus y. So if we write&nbsp;
that out algebraically, what that tells you is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:42.060
x minus y equals something - let's call it a - times&nbsp;
2 to the m. Because if this is going to be a factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:48.120
of that there has to be something else. So in our&nbsp;
previous example we had this was 96, we had 32 here,

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:54.840
so a would be 3. It's just that other integer part&nbsp;
of our division. So if 2 to the m divides that it,

00:10:54.840 --> 00:11:00.660
means that. So if I now say, well what's y minus&nbsp;
x? Well that's just minus a times 2 to the m. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:05.700
the same power of 2 still divides the number the&nbsp;
other way around, we've just got a minus sign in front.

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:09.840
We don't care about that, we're in the integers so&nbsp;
we're allowed negative numbers. So-
- (So positivity)

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:17.040
(could go out the window there)
- Positivity does still&nbsp;
hold, because the function itself was the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:24.060
power of 2 that divides the number. So even though&nbsp;
there's a minus here, this- so this might be &nbsp;

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:31.620
-96, the biggest power of 2 is 32. So it's still the&nbsp;
biggest power of 2 multiplied by some integer, now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:37.020
it's a negative one, but the power of 2 is still&nbsp;
positive so we're okay. So that gives us symmetry;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:43.860
now triangle law, a little bit of work required.&nbsp;
Let's start by thinking about (x,y). Suppose this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:50.357
is 1 over 2 to the m, and let's suppose the same&nbsp;
power also divides this one, 1 over 2 to the&nbsp;m as well.

00:11:50.357 --> 00:11:55.560
So we're sort of thinking here, what is&nbsp;
the largest power of 2 that divides both of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.560 --> 00:12:00.600
two okay? And pick that one to be our distance for&nbsp;
these two. Because what we can then say is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:09.000
means x minus y is equal to uh some number - let's&nbsp;
call it a - times 2 to the m. This one means y&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:15.480
minus z is some number b times 2 to the m. These of&nbsp;
course could have powers of 2 in them, there could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:19.800
be a bigger power of 2 that divides this one,&nbsp;
but we want a power of 2 that works on both of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:25.260
them. And then if we try and work out this one- so&nbsp;
for here we have to consider x minus z. So what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:33.120
x minus z? We've got equations for things here so&nbsp;
we can say x minus z is- x is going to be y plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:38.520
a(2 to the m). And then we want to subtract off z,
we've got z - if I take it over there and do that - z is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:44.520
y minus b(2 to the m). So x minus z we're going to&nbsp;
lose the ys and we've got minus minus becomes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:51.420
plus, so what we've got is a plus b times 2 to the&nbsp;
m. So what this is saying is if I have a power of 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:58.560
that divides x minus y, and a power- the same power&nbsp;
of 2 that also divides y and z, then it definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:03.660
divides x minus z. And it can't get smaller;&nbsp;
if you sort of look at what's happening here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:09.120
because it divides these two, by this algebra it's&nbsp;
definitely a factor of this one. So it definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:16.200
divides that one. But it can get bigger, the power&nbsp;
of 2 here could get bigger. Because a plus b, as I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:20.160
mentioned here, these could have factors of 2 in.&nbsp;
So when you add them together this could contain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:27.540
additional powers of 2, but that would just mean&nbsp;
this one had a larger power of 2 - what's our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:33.780
definition of distance? It's 1 over that power.
So you could potentially have a bigger power of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:39.420
2 dividing x minus z, but that means the distance&nbsp;
between x and z is smaller which is what we needed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:44.220
So that one's a little bit harder to get your&nbsp;
head around; and I recommend working through&nbsp;&nbsp;

