00:16:40.140 --> 00:16:46.200
1 - so it's actually a nice and easy 10 to the n minus 1&nbsp;
is our nth term. So we need to show this and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:52.260
tells us that this sequence does indeed go to -1.&nbsp;
What's our definition of distance we're using? The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:57.300
2-adic metric; because if we use the real numbers,&nbsp;
it obviously is infinity, that's no fun. We wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.300 --> 00:17:05.820
to get -1. So we need to find the largest power of&nbsp;
2 which divides that number minus that number.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:12.600
So of course -1 plus 1 10 to the n. So what's&nbsp;
the largest power of 2 which divides 10 to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.600 --> 00:17:19.380
n? Well 10 to the n is 2 to the n times 5 to the&nbsp;
n; because 10 is 2 times 5, 2 to the n is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:24.600
a factor so our distance in the 2-adic metric&nbsp;
between the nth term of the sequence to the limit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:33.240
-1 is 1 divided by 2 to the n. What happens&nbsp;
to this is n tends to infinity? 1 over a bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:40.860
and bigger and bigger number, this tends to zero&nbsp;
as n tends to infinity. So what this means is n&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:47.340
tends to infinity is saying as the number in the&nbsp;
sequence - 10 to the n minus 1 - as this number gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:52.260
bigger and bigger and bigger, more and more 9s&nbsp;
in a row, the distance between this really big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.260 --> 00:18:00.840
number and -1 gets smaller and smaller. Because&nbsp;
in this measure of distance, the 2-adic space, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:06.180
this space the way that we measure the distance is&nbsp;
1 over the biggest power of 2 that divides it.&nbsp;

00:18:06.180 --> 00:18:12.180
So this number is getting huge and it's getting&nbsp;
closer and closer to -1 in this space so we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:17.700
say the sequence of 9s converges to -1.
- (Does this&nbsp;happen with a lot of other sequences? Like is this-)

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:22.800
(is this some unique property of numbers with&nbsp;
lots of 9s in it and the -1? Or could you pull-)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:28.080
(could you manufacture other sequences where this&nbsp;happens?)
- You can do this with any sequence you want.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:34.800
So the 9s I just picked because it works quite&nbsp;
nicely with the algebra. The other thing you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:42.900
change is here we've done on a 2-adic metric, the&nbsp;
more general case is p-adic where p is a prime.

00:18:44.280 --> 00:18:51.540
So you need a prime number for lots of reasons&nbsp;
but that again is, I'm going to say, a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:56.580
beyond what I'm trying to get across here in this&nbsp;
particular case. But as long as you pick any prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.580 --> 00:19:01.500
number you can measure distance in the same way.&nbsp;
So you- for the 3-adic you would say it's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.500 --> 00:19:07.680
biggest power of 3 that divides the difference&nbsp;
between these two numbers and then your distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:14.280
is 1 over 3 to that power, similar kind&nbsp;of idea. 
- (So if you'd done 8 - 88, 888 and used)

00:19:14.280 --> 00:19:19.500
(8s instead of 9s would this necessarily still&nbsp;
work? Does it depend on the numbers?)
- No that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:24.480
work. I think that- off the top of my head I want to&nbsp;
say that would be zero because 8's perfectly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.480 --> 00:19:30.900
divisible by 2. If you change the numbers&nbsp;
in your sequence the limit will change. So -1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:37.560
here isn't special, that is just the limit for the&nbsp;
sequence with all the 9s. If you had a sequence with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:42.180
all the 8s like you said you're going to get&nbsp;
a different limit - and it might not even converge.&nbsp;

00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:47.760
It doesn't have to converge to something. Like&nbsp;
because I could write down an arbitrary sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:53.700
that's not going to necessarily converge. So you do&nbsp;
have to be careful, it needs to be something which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:59.580
almost has been like artificially constructed to&nbsp;
make sure it's going to converge, and then you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.580 --> 00:20:05.280
to figure out what that limit is. And finding that&nbsp;
limit can be quite tricky. So I told you it was -1&nbsp;&nbsp;

